you  [Music]  this week marks the 50th anniversary of  the assassination of dr. Martin Luther  King jr. a new CBS News poll out just  this morning shows 53 percent of  Americans believe only some of dr.  King's goals have been achieved we'll  examine dr. King's legacy all this week  but we'll begin today with remembering  Martin Luther King jr. the father only  on CBS Michelle Miller spoke with his  children for their first joint interview  in 12 years Michelle is in Birmingham  Alabama where King led some of his most  successful nonviolent protests against  racial inequality back in 1963 Michelle  good morning good morning well dr.  Martin Luther King jr. was both a pastor  and a father of four young children  during those protests to liberate  Birmingham Martin the third Dexter and  Bernice were just 10 seven and five  years old when the father died five  years later but the trauma of April 4th  1968 still haunts them Martin and I were  watching television and even to this day  when I see a breaking news flash I I  have PTSD and you you see your father  being shot on television that that's  very very traumatic dr. Martin Luther  King the apostle of non-violence and the  civil rights movement has been shot to  death in Memphis Tennessee  I remember us going back to mother and  dad's room to get an explanation from  her I don't know that she said your  daddy has been shot  what I remember her saying was your dad  has gone home to live with God have you  grieved in the way that we all grieve  the loss of a loved one all of us to an  extent have not fully had the chance to  grieve I modeled my mom our mom was so  stoic and she held everything together  for all of us to be strong but as a  child when you emulate that in a real  sense your whole  in your emotions have you let go I'm  still working on it  it's almost a lifelong process my first  real moment was on a retreat with our  youth how old were you I was 16 laid out  over a bed for two hours I kept saying  why you know why like why did you leave  me why why did you you know take him did  you blame anyone  I was angry with God because I felt he  could have stopped it I was angry at my  father you know for leaving me I was  angry at white people because I felt  like they were responsible and I didn't  know at the time whether I was angry at  my mother and me  eventually I realized I had anger at  everybody I want to show you something  Mike Wallace young sitting down with  your family the Christmas after the  assassination of your father do you ever  have any difficulties because you are  Martin Luther King sometimes I have  difficulties one time not too long ago a  boy it was teasing me about my name too  good now and what do you want for  Christmas money a bicycle what did that  bring back to you watching that that  interview every time I see something out  of our childhood I kind of longed for  that time period where we don't have to  have so much responsibility you were  very close  yeah as kids we were yeah are you in a  good place now  was there a rift I don't think that I  can recall that there was a time that we  got to the place where we never spoke to  each other  I think publicly you have to be allowed  to work through and navigate through  your challenges did you have to lose  your father for the nation in the world  to appreciate him the way they do today  I had to do it all over again what I  want my dad here I would say no no our  world is in a better place because our  father gave his life  yes I walked through yeah they had here  dad they're having him you know to ask  the tough questions when I'm going out  on a date or something but you know  where would our world be the king  children say they believe that their  fathers legacy has actually been dumbed  down they say that he was far more  radical in his ideas and his quest to  end racial and economic inequality not  to mention the Vietnam War and it's of  course Gayle you know that it was his  support for those striking sanitation  workers that led him to Memphis in 1968  and ultimately to his death yeah I still  remember now looking at the newsreel it  says I am a man where he went to to to  advocate on behalf of the sanitation  workers but it's great Michelle that you  got all three of them to sit down  together you very rarely see that with  the three of them that's really nice yes  they have their healing and they're in a  good place as they said all right  Michelle thank you very much we all hear  about Martin Luther King for some people  he's just a name he's just a speech but  you forget he was a father and it was  interesting to hear what his children  hear Bernice even said she was angry  with him too moving

